Cross-repo semantic baseline for the lume host tree.

WHY THIS EXISTS
  lume-core's tests/*-consistency.lume suites compare THREE backends
  (interpreter / text IR / LLVM) running the same script. The lume host has
  no native emitter or compiler backend: it builds a single interpreter
  binary from lang/ (a pinned copy of core), with agent-httpd for HTTP/DB.
  There is therefore no "three-backend consistency" to test here, and
  copying tests/native-consistency.lume (1560 lines, core-only builtins such
  as crypt_verify / chisq_cdf) would be both wrong and unbuildable.

  The real risk on the host side is different: HOST_PINNED files
  (typecheck.c, value.c, interp.c, ...) are hand-merged from core, so a
  partial merge can silently change language semantics without any md5
  drift in the synced files. This baseline pins the *observable behaviour*
  of the host interpreter byte-for-byte against core's interpreter.

FILES (byte-identical copies of core fixtures, do not hand-edit)
  baseline-literals-bitops.lume  <- lume-core/tests/literals-bitops-consistency.lume
  baseline-block-scope.lume     <- lume-core/tests/p1-9-consistency.lume
  Copied at lume-core fabe9e2b8575461071e2507d9cfb2f04ce9a1876.
  Both are pure-language fixtures: no imports, no server, no HTTP/DB/FS,
  no core-only builtins, deterministic output.

  baseline-*.expected are the authoritative outputs produced by
  bin/lume-core (the core interpreter), with CR stripped. tests/run_all.sh
  runs bin/lume on each fixture and diffs against these files.

HOW IT IS KEPT HONEST
  .github/workflows/ci.yml (check-sync job) cmp's the two .lume copies
  against the sibling lume-core checkout. If core edits a fixture and the
  host copy is not refreshed, CI fails instead of quietly testing stale
  semantics. Re-sync by copying the core file and regenerating the matching
  .expected with the core interpreter; verify bin/lume still matches.

  This is deliberately NOT part of lang/PIN.manifest: these are host-owned
  test assets, not synced runtime sources.
